**Ternary expression tree.**

Define an abstract base class and let each node evaluate itself, so there is no `switch` on node type:

```cpp
struct Expr { virtual ~Expr() = default; virtual int eval() const = 0; };
struct Num : Expr { int v; explicit Num(int v):v(v){} int eval() const override { return v; } };
struct Bin : Expr {                      // e.g. +, -, <, ==
    char op; std::unique_ptr<Expr> l, r; /* ctor omitted */
    int eval() const override { int a=l->eval(), b=r->eval();
        switch(op){case '+':return a+b; case '<':return a<b; /*...*/} return 0; } };
struct Ternary : Expr {
    std::unique_ptr<Expr> cond, yes, no;
    int eval() const override { return cond->eval() ? yes->eval() : no->eval(); }
};
```

Points: polymorphism through virtual `eval`; `Ternary` evaluates only the chosen branch (short-circuit, important when a branch has side effects or divides by zero); virtual destructor and smart pointers for ownership; adding a new node type is a new subclass (open-closed). Alternatives: visitor pattern when many operations (print, evaluate, optimise) apply to the same tree. Complexity: O(n) time for n nodes, O(h) stack depth for tree height h, which could overflow for degenerate chains. If parsing is needed, a recursive-descent parser handles right-associativity of `?:`.

**Design a timer.** Requirements: `schedule(delay, callback) -> id`, `cancel(id)`, optional repeating. Core: a min-heap (priority queue) keyed by expiry time, a worker thread that sleeps on a condition variable until the earliest deadline (or until a new earlier task arrives), runs due callbacks, and a map for cancellation (lazy deletion flag). Use a monotonic clock. Insert O(log n), fire O(log n). Decisions: run callbacks on the timer thread or a thread pool (long callbacks delay others), thread safety with one mutex, and shutdown handling. For huge timer counts, a hashed timing wheel gives O(1) insert and tick.
